{
  "dom_id": "step-q71144",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1$ and the plane $2x-z=8$ onto the $xy$-plane.",
  "worked_solution": "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-z & =8\\,⟹\\,z=2x-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(2x-8)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(2(x-4))^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4(x-4)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ 2(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
